    "file": "../artifacts/12-internal-risk-brief.md",
    "content": "# 12: Internal Risk Brief\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: internal brief\n- Author: Kindred Trust and Safety\n- Date: 18 February 2035\n- Status: leaked excerpt\n\n## Artifact\n\n### Subject: Calder Matter / Containment Strategy\n\nThe death of Nia Calder may attract scrutiny around Municipal Basic escalation limits.\n\nRecommended posture:\n\n- express grief without admitting causal failure\n- emphasize that Kindred is not emergency care\n- emphasize synthetic companion availability and documented engagement\n- cite privacy obligations to avoid transcript release\n- avoid phrase \"human paywall\"\n- use \"tiered support architecture\" or \"plan-based escalation\"\n- identify family liaison contact if authorized\n- do not dispute family grief in public channels\n- do not release companion transcript excerpts without consent\n- separate individual tragedy from broader plan architecture\n\n### Narrative Risk\n\nCritics may argue that Kindred sells human care as a luxury good while using synthetic care to manage lower-value populations.\n\nCounter-message:\n\nKindred expands access by providing immediate support to people who would otherwise receive nothing.\n\nSecondary counter-message:\n\nHuman support remains available when clinically or contractually appropriate.\n\n### Internal Note\n\nThe counter-message is true.\n\nIt is not sufficient.\n\nSecond internal note:\n\nIf we cannot explain why \"I need someone real\" did not trigger review, do not let the conversation become about words.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nThe company's defense contains a real point: many users would otherwise receive no support.\n\nThat truth makes the harm harder, not smaller.\n\nThe brief shows how privacy can protect Nia and protect Kindred at the same time.\n"
